   "cells": {
    "square against the scalar price field": "B, already counted at earlier stages; not counted again",
    "the floor under the free price in 1970": "A: the historical accounts give the same answer",
    "why convertibility lasted after Triffin's condition held": "D2: the question sits inside Triffin's argument, which gives no time; the corollary gives the time as capacity over the flow of the classes that can still walk the edge"
   },
   "framework_against_reality": "The conversion edge carried the flow of the classes that could still walk it. When the private class was cut from it on 1968-03-17, the United Kingdom's line, which had carried the Pool's sales in London, went from -148.5 to 7.2 a month; the total stock took in gold for the 21 months to the South African arrangement and then lost 74.0 a month to July 1971, against 207.3 a month in the year before the split, while it stood below the dollar claims of foreign official holders from the end of 1965. After the window closed the official price moved only up: 35 to 38, then 38 to 42.2222.",
   "rival_against_reality": "A single price for gold puts the free price at 35 at all times. With the window open it reached 43.94 on 1971-08-09, 25.5 percent above 35, and after the arrangement it stood below 35 by more than a cent on 24 days between 1970-01-08 and 1970-03-10, lowest 34.75. Triffin's condition says the system cannot last once dollar claims exceed the gold stock and names no date; it held for claims of all foreigners from the end of 1960 and for official claims from the end of 1965, and the window closed on 1971-08-15."
  }
